Add assign_server(): a Server-Assignment-Request of a chosen type from the cfg.

The only SAR the script could send for a request other than REGISTER was
assign_server_unreg(), which is UNREGISTERED_USER: it downloads the profile and
tells the HSS the user is unregistered at this S-CSCF. That is the right thing
for a terminating request to an unregistered user, and the wrong one for the
case TS 23.380 4.4.2 is about -- an S-CSCF that restarted and lost its usrloc
receiving an originating request from a UE that is still registered. There the
S-CSCF is to fetch the profile with NO_ASSIGNMENT, which changes nothing at the
HSS and which the HSS answers only for the S-CSCF already assigned to the user
(DIAMETER_UNABLE_TO_COMPLY otherwise, TS 29.228 6.1.2.1), and continue the
request as normal. There was no way to send it.

assign_server(async_route, domain, direction, type) is assign_server_unreg()
with the type as a fourth parameter, and assign_server_unreg() is now that with
UNREGISTERED_USER. Two types are accepted:

  0  NO_ASSIGNMENT      the profile is stored; an IMPU already in usrloc keeps
                        its registration state and bindings, a new one is
                        created IMPU_UNREGISTERED -- a profile and no binding,
                        which is what the usrloc timer makes of any record
                        without a valid contact anyway.
  3  UNREGISTERED_USER  as before.

REGISTRATION and RE_REGISTRATION belong to save(). The de-registration types
would have the HSS forget an assignment while usrloc keeps its bindings, since
nothing here tears them down, so they are refused.

The SAA callback decided whether to send the REGISTER's 200 and whether usrloc
was already updated by comparing the type with UNREGISTERED_USER. That is now a
flag on the saved transaction, set by assign_server() -- NO_ASSIGNMENT is the
type save() itself starts from before it picks the real one, so the type is not
a safe marker for "sent from the script".

The Restoration-Info half of 23.380 (S-CSCF backing up Path and Contact at the
HSS and restoring bindings from them) is deliberately not part of this.

